WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.174
Data is everywhere, even on web pages.

2
00:00:03.174 --> 00:00:07.921
In this lesson, you will learn to load a table straight from a website,

3
00:00:07.921 --> 00:00:13.686
turn it into a table visual, and format and sort it so the key values stand out.

4
00:00:13.686 --> 00:00:16.060
Let's bring the web into Power BI!

5
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:18.805
Our goal:

6
00:00:18.805 --> 00:00:24.840
a small table of currency exchange rates, loaded from a web page, and formatted beautifully.

7
00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:38.590
With Get data from the Web, Power BI reads the page, lists the tables

8
00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:41.499
it finds, and loads the one we choose.

9
00:00:41.499 --> 00:00:43.680
While it loads, you see: Evaluating.

10
00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:05.132
The new table appears in the Data pane.

11
00:01:05.132 --> 00:01:09.080
I add a Table visual, and tick the currency and rate columns.

12
00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:25.620
Now the fun part: formatting!

13
00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:30.708
Select the visual, open the Format pane with the paintbrush, and explore:

14
00:01:30.708 --> 00:01:34.100
style presets, grid, values, column headers, and totals.

15
00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:57.620
Under Values, I make the font bigger and clearer, so the numbers are easy to read.

16
00:01:57.620 --> 00:02:00.820
And I give the visual more space on the canvas.

17
00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:20.159
Good habit:

18
00:02:20.159 --> 00:02:22.465
always check your numbers against the source.

19
00:02:22.465 --> 00:02:25.100
So I compare the rates with the website.

20
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:40.982
Back in Power BI, click a column header to sort.

21
00:02:40.982 --> 00:02:43.420
Now the highest rates are on top.

22
00:02:55.500 --> 00:03:02.040
A readable font, a smart sort, and clear headers turn a simple table into a clear story. Awesome work!

